---
// src/pages/index.astro — the Astro half: mount the island, nothing else.
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ThemeIsland from "../components/ThemeIsland";
import FamilySwitcher from "../components/FamilySwitcher";
---

<BaseLayout>
  <main>
    <h1>Themvora · Astro + React</h1>

    <p class="muted">
      Interactive themed UI lives inside the island. The page itself stays
      Astro.
    </p>

    <ThemeIsland client:load />
    <FamilySwitcher client:load />

    <section class="surface">
      <h2>What this page demonstrates</h2>

      <p>
        The overlay scrollbar is mounted by the layout (<code
          >&lt;ThemeScrollbar /&gt;</code
        >), not by the island, and the layout pairs it with <code
          >scrollbar</code
        > on the provider so the native bar is hidden from the first frame instead
        of after hydration.
      </p>

      <p>
        It has to work on a cold load, with no interaction: the overlay reads
        every colour from the theme runtime, so it needs a runtime before the
        visitor touches anything. The island installs one when it hydrates; the
        component installs the app-wide runtime itself when the page has none,
        which is the case on any page that mounts the overlay without an island.
      </p>

      <p>
        This page is deliberately taller than the viewport — a scrollbar you
        cannot scroll demonstrates nothing.
      </p>

      <h2>Try it</h2>

      <ol>
        <li>
          Scroll this page before touching a control. The themed rail is already
          there.
        </li>
        <li>
          Toggle the theme in the island. The rail re-colours; it is not
          rebuilt.
        </li>
        <li>
          Reload. The rail is back on the first frame, with the theme you left.
        </li>
        <li>
          Follow the link to <a href="/native">the native controls page</a> and toggle
          from there.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Why it is split this way</h2>

      <p>
        Astro renders the document; the island owns the interactive panel. The
        two share one runtime, published on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, so a
        native control and a React control can never disagree about the
        selection.
      </p>

      <p>
        Nothing here is a workaround for the scrollbar. No theme toggle, no
        second load, no file save: a normal page load initializes it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p>
      <a href="/native">Native controls, same runtime</a>
    </p>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
